# RME PRE-CONSENT UI CAPABILITY FOUNDATION

BUGFIX-RME-PRECONSENT-FIRST-PAGE-UI-GATE-1 — durable rules.

Companion to `134-odontogram-pre-consent-authoring.mdc`, which owns the
odontogram side of the same encounter. Rule 134 §6 already required the
odontogram page to agree with the server. This rule states the same requirement
for the RME side, where it was not enforced and a live mismatch reached
production.

## 1. The rule

The active RME authoring UI must mirror the **server-side RME authoring
capability**.

After a Doctor starts an examination but before a valid same-patient,
same-visit Consent exists, active RME authoring remains forbidden. When no RME
page exists in that state, the UI must **not** offer an actionable
"Buat Halaman RM Pertama" control.

Once a valid same-visit Consent exists, the first-page create action may be
offered, subject to every other RME authoring guard.

UI visibility is **not** a security boundary. Direct RME creation stays
protected by the canonical server-side service guard, and remains protected
whatever the page renders.

This rule does **not** alter the odontogram rule: after examination start the
authorized Doctor may still author the active Native Odontogram before Consent.

This rule does **not** alter the finish rule: valid Consent remains required
before "Selesai Pemeriksaan".

## 2. The matrix

Extends the rule 134 matrix with the column that was missing — what the page is
allowed to OFFER, as distinct from what the server allows.

| state | Odontogram | RME edit | RME first-page CTA | Selesai Pemeriksaan |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `registered` / `waiting` | DENY | DENY | **NOT ACTIONABLE** | DENY |
| `in_progress`, consent UNSIGNED | ALLOW | DENY | **NOT ACTIONABLE** | DENY |
| `in_progress`, consent VALID | ALLOW | ALLOW | **ACTIONABLE** | ALLOW (other guards apply) |
| `cashier_pending` / `completed` | DENY | DENY | NOT ACTIONABLE | n/a |
| `cancelled` | DENY | DENY | no workspace at all (404) | n/a |
| historical native sheet | DENY | DENY | NOT ACTIONABLE | n/a |
| published Legacy RME | DENY | DENY (immutable) | n/a | n/a |

## 3. What went wrong, so it is not repeated

The empty-state workspace derived its create control from
`can('create', [MedicalRecord::class, $visit])`.
`MedicalRecordPolicy::create` answers a **narrower** question than the button
implied: may this user manage RME for this branch and this patient. It says
nothing about consent — correctly, because **consent is not a permission**. It
is a property of the encounter, resolved by
`RmeVisitConsentService::canAuthorRmeForPatient()`.

So the POPULATED workspace and the EMPTY workspace read two different truths
about one act, and the empty one offered a create the server then refused.

The lesson generalises past this button: **a permission check is not a
capability check.** Any control whose act is gated by encounter state, consent,
or workflow status must consult the capability, not only the permission.

## 4. Where it is enforced

`RmeVisitConsentService::assertRmeAuthoringAllowedForPatient()` is the single
authority for every current-RME write, and
`canAuthorRmeForPatient()` is its **side-effect-free** presentation twin. Both
resolve the same four conditions:

1. the patient HAS a single current encounter;
2. that encounter is `in_progress`;
3. the actor may work on it (branch scope + clinical scope);
4. a valid signed consent exists for THAT SAME encounter.

`MedicalRecordService::createDraft()` asserts it, so a crafted POST is refused
regardless of what any page rendered. The controller passes the predicate to the
view; the view renders from it.

**Never re-derive the rule in Blade.** A template that writes its own
`$visit->consent && $visit->consent->signed_at` has created a second rule that
will drift from the first — which is the failure this sprint exists to close.
The value arrives as a controller-provided key or it is not used.

## 5. What "not actionable" means

Rendering a **disabled** control alongside the reason is preferred over hiding
it. The doctor stays oriented — the action exists, it is simply not available
yet — and learns what to do next rather than discovering the lock by pressing.

What must NOT survive is the submittable path: no `<form>` pointed at the store
route, and no live submit button. Do not leave an enabled control that relies on
the backend failing to enforce the rule.

The refusal must name the real reason, matching the populated workspace's
wording: "Belum ada pemeriksaan yang berjalan" when there is no encounter, and
"Persetujuan Tindakan Medis belum ditandatangani" when the signature is
outstanding, with the link to sign it.

## 6. Never resolve a mismatch by weakening the server

When UI and backend disagree about an act, the correct direction is
**UI DENY + BACKEND DENY**. Relaxing the service so the offered control starts
working is a clinical-authorization regression wearing the clothes of a
consistency fix.

## 7. Testing

- Prove the SERVER rule server-side: assert the service/HTTP outcome.
- Prove the UI claim on the **rendered output**, not on a helper boolean — and
  assert on the submittable form, never on the label. A disabled button still
  reads "Buat Halaman RM Pertama", and that is the state we want.
- `tests/Feature/RME/RmePreConsentFirstPageUiGateTest.php` is the pinned suite.
  It is declared in `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites` and
  carries the `RmePreConsentFirstPageUiGate` token in every critical gate
  variant, so a rename must move the coverage and can never silently drop it.
- Any change here must keep pinning the neighbours it could take with it: the
  odontogram still editable pre-consent, finish still refused pre-consent,
  before-start still refused, other-Doctor and non-Doctor still refused,
  historical visits still closed, and another visit's or another patient's
  consent never unlocking the control.
